          <div class="nb-section">
            <h4 class="nb-title">1. Rotate Operations and Multi-Word Shifts</h4>
            <p>
              A rotate moves bits from one end of a register or memory location back around to the other end, so no bits are lost, unlike a shift:
            </p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>ROL and ROR:</strong> Rotate directly, end to end. The bit shifted out of one end wraps around to the other end and is also copied into the Carry Flag (CF). Example: <code>ROL SI, 14</code> rotates SI left 14 places. 4-bit rotate (<code>ROL AL, 4</code>) performs a nibble swap.</li>
              <li><strong>RCL and RCR:</strong> Rotate through the carry flag, treating carry as an extra 9th bit (for byte) or 17th bit (for word) in the rotation ring.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="nb-card" style="margin-top: 10px;">
              <h5>Shifting a 48-Bit Number Left, One Place (Week 5 Slide 38)</h5>
              <p>
                A 48-bit value spread across <code>DX:BX:AX</code> shifts left as one unit by chaining <code>SHL</code> with two <code>RCL</code> instructions, carrying each register's overflow bit into the next:
              </p>
              <pre><code>SHL AX, 1     ; AX's top bit moves into carry
RCL BX, 1     ; carry moves into BX's bottom bit; BX's top bit moves into carry
RCL DX, 1     ; carry moves into DX's bottom bit</code></pre>
              <p><em>Check:</em> The 48-bit value doubled, exactly as a single left shift should. Before: <code>0001:8000:0001</code> &rarr; After: <code>0003:0000:0002</code>.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
